 \section{Discussion}
 
-PPI graph augmentation improved benchmark performance in the shared-graph evaluation, with effects that varied by evaluation design and baseline choice. PPI-GCN improved over raw ESM in the original split, and the gain was sensitive to protein-topology correspondence and cross-split connectivity. With \CADDAM{} included, raw ESM, edge-ablated PPI-GCN and topology-permuted PPI-GCN all produced large fusion gains, while real PPI-GCN was only modestly higher. The strong-predictor fusion gain primarily reflects protein-representation complementarity, with a smaller real-topology margin.
-
-Under the homology-aware split, PPI-GCN remained useful relative to raw ESM. The retained graph-fusion estimate was lower than the repeated-run architecture-matched \CADDAM{} baseline when homologous protein clusters were kept within partitions. The prevalence shift defines this analysis as a stress test. Graph complementarity depends on the baseline predictor, split design and graph-control baseline.
-
-Graph propagation changed predictions in structured topology contexts. The observed patterns may reflect differences between proteins embedded in densely connected functional neighbourhoods and those occupying more boundary-like network positions. Independent cohorts, temporal ClinVar splits, disease-specific networks and alternative PPI sources can test these candidate explanations.
-
-The evaluation has several constraints. ClinVar labels may reflect submission bias and future label updates \cite{Landrum2020ClinVar}. Unknown historical overlap with the development data of CADD and AlphaMissense limits prospective interpretation of their benchmark scores. Protein-level mapping omits isoform-specific effects and transcript ambiguity. STRING combined scores integrate several evidence channels and extend beyond direct physical interactions \cite{Szklarczyk2023STRING}. The transductive split and direct-edge ablation retain train-validation, validation-test and higher-order paths. Validation AUC guided checkpoint selection, and the retained PPI-GCN run showed a larger validation gain than test gain; the conclusions draw on held-out test metrics, repeated-seed summaries and protein-clustered uncertainty. The homology-aware prevalence shift limits cross-split AUPRC comparison. Figure 4 transition counts depend on a fixed decision-score threshold, and class-weighted sigmoid outputs are not calibrated clinical probabilities. All fusion controls used identical preprocessing, though alternative feature scaling may change absolute performance. The audit uses retained processed outputs and a compact source-data archive, with provider-controlled resources available from their original sources. Within these constraints, PPI graph augmentation provides context-dependent network information alongside strong sequence and variant-effect predictors.
+PPI graph augmentation improved benchmark performance in the shared-graph evaluation, with effects that varied by evaluation design and baseline choice. The historical and matched audits show sensitivity to graph connectivity, but protein-level uncertainty limits the full-versus-raw claim. With \CADDAM{} included, protein-representation controls retained much of the gain, while the current real-minus-raw fusion margin was small and positive. These results support representation complementarity with a limited additional STRING margin under the original split.
+
+Under the homology-aware split, the historical graph-fusion point was below the repeated score baseline, but the current matched graph-versus-score interval spanned zero. Additional input weights, redundant high-dimensional features, overfitting, feature scaling and changed protein composition are possible explanations for weaker fusion; these mechanisms were not experimentally separated. The prevalence shift limits cross-split AUPRC comparisons. A stable harmful graph effect cannot be inferred from the retained single run.
+
+Graph propagation changed predictions in heterogeneous topology contexts. The observed profiles may reflect densely connected functional neighbourhoods or boundary-like network roles, but their post-hoc selection requires independent confirmation. External HUMSAVAR validation supports STRING transfer under the stated exclusions; BioGRID's uncertain incremental gains limit generalization across networks. Both databases may share underlying evidence, and the networks overlap. This is not independent-patient or prospective validation.
+
+The evaluation has several constraints. ClinVar labels may reflect submission bias and future updates \cite{Landrum2020ClinVar}. Unknown development-data overlap for CADD and AlphaMissense limits prospective interpretation. Protein-level mapping omits isoform and residue-specific effects. STRING associations extend beyond direct physical interactions \cite{Szklarczyk2023STRING}; transductive propagation and direct-edge ablation retain indirect cross-partition paths. Outputs from class-weighted training are not calibrated clinical probabilities, and transition counts depend on the chosen threshold. The external set excludes specified variants/proteins but not all homologues or shared annotation evidence; external clinical-score fusion was not evaluated. These findings concern fixed propagation and should not be generalized to trained graph neural networks.
+
+\paragraph{Practical recommendations}
+Compare graph features with raw embeddings and strong score baselines under matched training settings. State whether held-out proteins remain visible in the graph, assess uncertainty at the protein level, and treat selected topology groups as hypotheses. Check calibration and decision thresholds before clinical use.
 
